A full diagnostic of the path between PC15 and PC16 found no fault at any layer.
Both hosts are up with correctly configured IP addresses on the same /24 subnet
(172.18.51.15 and 172.18.51.16 on 172.18.51.0/24). Their network interfaces
(eth0) are physically UP with no RX/TX errors or drops. ARP resolution is
working correctly in both directions — each host has the other's MAC address
cached and marked REACHABLE. At the switch layer, switch10 ports eth1 (PC15)
and eth2 (PC16) are both in VLAN 1 with bridge state "forwarding"; no ebtables
or iptables filtering rules are present on the switch or on either endpoint.
Bidirectional ICMP ping tests confirm 0% packet loss and sub-2 ms round-trip
times in both directions. The symptom described in the helpdesk ticket — "nothing
goes through at all" — is not reproducible under current lab conditions. The
most likely explanation is that the issue was transient (a brief STP
reconvergence, a momentary process crash that has since recovered, or a user
error at the time of the report). No corrective action is needed at the network
level. If the users are still experiencing trouble, the problem is likely
application-layer (e.g. a file-sharing daemon not running, a hostname resolution
failure, or a firewall on the host OS outside the lab environment), not a
network path failure.
